OK. For run resolution, the cleanest approach that handles 404 deterministically: `gh api repos/o/r/actions/runs/{id}` — on 404 gh CLI exits non-zero. Does cli_execute_command throw, or return the error? From patterns: `|| true` is appended where failure is tolerated (line 3734), and try/catch used elsewhere — suggesting it throws on failure. For a deleted run, gh api → throw with "gh: Could not resolve to a Run with the number of <id>" or 404. So in the resolver: catch the throw and treat as null ONLY if the error looks like a 404/not-found; other errors → rethrow (probe error → fail-safe live). That's fragile (message matching). 

Alternative: fetch the head's run LIST once (headWorkflowRunsSafe — all runs per_page=50) and resolve run ids from that map: a check-run whose run id is ABSENT from the head's run list ⇒ 'no-run' (404-equivalent: the run no longer exists — deleted runs drop off the list); a run with conclusion cancelled ⇒ 'cancelled'. This avoids per-run API calls entirely, reuses the existing probe, matches the #1457 evidence ("run no longer exists"), and is exactly what the ticket says: "whose run_id 404s or whose run is cancelled". Absent-from-list ≈ 404. 

Edge: pagination — per_page=50 caps; a head with >50 runs could hide an old ghost's run... but for zombie detection: if the run is not in the newest 50 of the head, and the check-run is still in_progress... could false-positive a zombie for a genuinely-running old run. Extremely unlikely (50 runs one head) and the consequence is one bounded re-dispatch. Note it.

But wait — there's a subtlety: the run list from `/actions/runs?head_sha=X` — does it include runs whose checks are on that head but with a different head_sha? Check runs registered on head sha X belong to runs whose head_sha IS X. OK.

Also runs deleted by retention: workflow runs can't be individually deleted via UI... actually they CAN ( Actions → run → "Delete workflow run"). The #1457 instance: "run no longer exists (html_url unresolvable)". So yes, deleted runs. Absent from list = deleted = 404-equivalent. 

So the resolver design:

```js
function headCheckRunZombies(repoInfo, headSha, opts) {
    // returns null when the probe family fails (caller keeps legacy behavior),
    // else { zombies: [{name, runId, reason}], live: n }
    var checkRuns = headCheckRunsSafe(repoInfo, headSha);
    if (!checkRuns) return null;
    var runs = headWorkflowRunsSafe(repoInfo, headSha);  // hmm — this adds a SECOND gh call per candidate...
```

Hmm — cost: validate_pr candidates already fetch headWorkflowRunsSafe (gh-748 race probe) but only at dispatch time (after the skipIfGreenCi guard). My zombie probe would run BEFORE the skip, adding 2 gh api calls per armed+green-cover candidate per tick. That's the price of detection; the gh-748 race probe could be reused if I reorder... Careful: don't refactor the world. Keep the two probes inside the zombie helper, executed only when skipIfGreenCi or latch-skip is about to fire (green cover path) — bounded to ~1 candidate/tick.

Alternatively resolve run ids against `runs?head_sha` filtered to workflow_dispatch only? No — ghosts may come from ANY cancelled run (kicker children are workflow_dispatch too, but a push-event run cancelled could also register checks). Use the full head rollup (headWorkflowRuns) like rerun_cancelled_checks does.

Wait, actually there's an even better existing source: `github_get_commit_check_runs` returns check runs each with a `check_suite`... does it include run id? The REST check-run object has `check_suite.id` (suite id, not run id) and `details_url` (contains run id). No direct run id field. details_url parsing it is.

Hmm, one more consideration: since headWorkflowRuns already returns runs with `id`, `status`, `conclusion`, `updated_at`, I can build the map directly.

Now the classification 'run-concluded-non-cancelled' case: in_progress check-run whose run completed successfully 10 min ago — propagation lag or dead bridge. Grace 5 min → zombie 'run-concluded'. This is a ghost too (the run's own check runs were registered by Actions itself and concluded; an in_progress one left behind = ghost). OK.

And 'no-run-link' stale case: bridge stamps carry no details_url at dispatch time (runUrl null)... wait, actually stampValidationChecksForModule is called post-dispatch with runUrl null → no details_url. So those check runs have no run link → the stale rule (30 min) classifies them. But careful — syncValidationChecks re-stamps with runUrl; those have links. And a LIVE validation's in_progress stamp (just dispatched, <30 min old) → live. Good. But what about the 15-min... the gh-748 race: a run dispatched seconds ago, its bridge stamp has no details_url → young → live. Good.

Now, the gh-766/mergeBot side? Out of scope — mergeBot never dispatches.

Where exactly in validate_pr to add:

```js
if (rule.skipIfGreenCi && vProbe && vProbe.green) {
    // gh-842: a green cover is VOID while the head carries zombie
    // check-runs ... probe → if zombies: fall through to re-dispatch
    ...
    console.log skip...
    continue;
}
```

becomes:

```js
if (rule.skipIfGreenCi && vProbe && vProbe.green) {
    var zGreen = headCheckRunZombies(effectiveRepoInfo, vHead0);
    if (zGreen && zGreen.zombies.length) {
        console.log('  🧟 ' + key + ' green cover void — ' + zGreen.zombies.length +
            ' zombie check-run(s) hold the gate (gh-842): ' + names + ' — re-dispatching');
        // fall through to the dispatch guards
    } else {
        console.log('  ⏭️  ' + key + ' head already carries a green dispatched CI run — ...');
        continue;
    }
```

And the latch-skip guard:

```js
if (rule.skipIfValidatedHead && vProbe && ai_validated && vProbe.green && validationRollupGreen(...)) {
    // gh-842: ghosts on the gate make an arm-only latch-skip a deadlock —
    // the gate never clears and no run is ever ordered. Probe; zombies → dispatch instead.
    var zLatch = headCheckRunZombies(...);
    if (zLatch && zLatch.zombies.length) {
        console.log('  🧟 ' + key + ' latch-skip void — zombie check-runs ... (gh-842) — dispatching instead');
        // fall through to dispatch path
    } else { ...existing latch-skip arm... }
```

Careful with the latch-skip fall-through: below it are deferRedHead, redHeadSkip, mutex re-check, gh-748 race, cancelStale, dispatch. Falling through from latch-skip must skip the arm-only branch — restructure with if/else.

Also vHead0 may be null → zombie probe skipped (guard `zGreen` null → keep legacy skip).

Cost concern: validationRollupGreen already creates a provider... fine.

Now the ORDER of run resolution: classify(checkRuns, runMapById, nowMs, opts). I'll pass the run list and let the module build the map? Module purity: pass `resolveRun` fn — tests inject. In smAgent, resolveRun looks up the map from headWorkflowRunsSafe; absent → null (no-run). But probe failure of headWorkflowRunsSafe returns null → then... can't distinguish "run not in list" from "list failed". If the head-runs probe fails, resolveRun can't prove anything → all run-linked pending check runs → fail-safe live? But no-run-link stamps still get stale-classified. Hmm: if the runs probe fails (null), I'll treat the whole zombie probe as failed → return null (legacy behavior, log warn). Simpler and conservative.

Edge: run in list with status 'queued' but the CHECK-run is in_progress... live. Good (AC2).

Now — should zombies also affect the OTHER consumers, e.g., merge-validated (checks green + CLEAN)? mergeState BLOCKED keeps it from matching; if mergeState reads CLEAN while ghosts exist (GitHub branch protection vs REST discrepancy — unlikely since BLOCKED came from protection), merge would be attempted and refused by the API... out of scope.

What about `computePrStatus` rollup reading 'pending' due to ghosts (the other live shape)? That routes to revalidate-armed (checks none|pending) → validate_pr without skipIfGreenCi → dispatches (guards pass) → heals. Already works. Good.

Now, the optional belt — skip it (note in response).

